I'd say both are true. For anything custom an agent wins, no question. But for the boring standard skeleton I like the deterministic version: schema-validated output, pinned actions, nothing hallucinated. And they combine nicely, an agent can run `make:ci` and edit from there. That's why `--no-interaction` matters so much to me.

That combination is exactly what I'm playing with in https://github.com/wachterjohannes/symfony-skills: a small skill would tell the agent to run `make:ci` with the right options instead of writing YAML from memory, and to touch the result only where the project differs from the standard. This maker would slot right in.
